# The ground: one grass tile, in four variants, laid under everything else.
#
# **40 x 40 texels a frame, at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 20**, which is 2.0 world
# units — `art::GROUND_TILE`, and the number the rest of this file follows from.
# A `.crpix` declares one frame size for the whole file, so the frame size *is*
# the tile size; there is no second place it could disagree with the code.
#
# # Where 2.0 comes from
#
# It is bounded on both sides and neither bound is aesthetic.
#
# Downwards by the sprite count. The ground is emitted per tile, so halving the
# tile roughly quadruples the quads for the same picture. At 2.0 a 960 x 720
# window asks for 300 tiles — the same order as the few hundred enemies a view
# holds, and a sixth of the 1 728 an arena-sized ground would be; halve it and
# the ground becomes the largest thing in the frame and the horde the small one.
# Both numbers are measured by
# `art::tests::the_visible_ground_is_bounded_by_the_view_and_not_the_arena`,
# which is also what keeps them honest when the view or the arena moves.
#
# Upwards by how much art a variant is. Every texel here is authored, and four
# variants of a 40-row tile is already 160 rows — about what `actors.crpix`
# spends on five actors. At 4.0 units it would be 320 rows, and the repeat unit
# would be four times the player's own body across: a tile you can see all of at
# once is a tile you can learn.
#
# 40 texels is also whole at 20 texels a unit, and 2.0 divides the arena exactly
# — 48 tiles across and 36 up. Nothing depends on that second fact, because the
# ground overruns the arena rather than stopping at it (see `art.rs`), but a
# tile size that left a fractional tile at the wall would have been one more
# thing to be careful about for no gain.
#
# # Four variants, and the tile has nothing in it bigger than a blade
#
# The failure to avoid is a ground that reads as a grid of one stamp, and
# **variant count is the weaker half of avoiding it.** The stronger half is that
# nothing drawn here has a shape: no blobs, no worn patches, no low-frequency
# mottle, nothing with a recognisable position inside the frame. A tile-scale
# feature is what lets an eye find the lattice — it recurs at exactly the tile
# pitch, in exactly the same place in the frame — and four variants do not hide
# one, they give it four places to be. So the whole tile is hollows, blades and
# the odd dry stem, every one of them one to four texels, laid over the frame
# including its edges so no border row reads as a seam.
#
# What is left for the variants to break is the *fine* repeat, and four is
# enough for that: `art::ground_variant` chooses with the top two bits of
# `crcbl::core::rand::hash_u64` over the tile's own coordinates, so a tile's
# choice is independent of its neighbours' and the shortest repeat in the ground
# is the hash's period rather than anything a player will stand next to.
#
# # It is darker and flatter than everything drawn on it, deliberately
#
# The whole palette lives between #19211a and #2f3f26 — five shades inside a
# tenth of the luminance range, all of them far below any actor. `actors.crpix`
# gives the player a bright rim specifically so it can be found in a crowd of
# hundreds at the same size, and a ground with contrast of its own is what takes
# that away. The green is also pulled towards olive rather than towards the XP
# gem's #4fe08c, which is the other thing on the field the eye has to pick out
# of a dark background.
#
# Neither half is left to the eye.
# `art::tests::the_grass_is_dimmer_and_flatter_than_the_player_it_carries`
# measures this file's luma against the baked player frame's, so a repalette
# that brightened the grass fails there instead of leaving this paragraph
# quietly wrong.
#
# # No `None`
#
# Nothing here is transparent. The ground is the bottom layer and covers the
# whole view, so a transparent texel is the clear colour showing through a lawn
# — `art::tests::the_ground_bakes_to_four_opaque_tiles` is what says there is
# not one. That is also why this file declares no `. c None` entry at all: an
# unused transparent key is one paste away from being used.

crpix: 40 40 4 5 1

palette:
  b c #202a1b   # the base, and most of a tile
  d c #19211a   # the hollow between two tufts
  m c #27331f   # a blade
  l c #2f3f26   # its lit tip, and the brightest thing on the ground
  e c #2a2519   # a dry stem

